%% DM26-0670

%% What the broker does when a publisher is faster than it can hold, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/cassini-backpressure.md,
%% and cassini-broker.md "The bounds, as built"). Every payload byte the broker holds is charged to one budget, whose limit is set at run
%% time from what the machine has. A slow subscriber is the other half, in broker/slow_subscriber_stall.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Domain as Domain actor
    participant Facade as Client facade (credit, outbox, buffer)
    participant Reader as the connection's reader
    participant Window as Window (a quarter of the budget)
    participant Budget as Budget
    participant Pressure as PressureManager
    participant Topic as TopicAgent

    Note over Domain,Facade: The client bounds itself first, by what the broker has not yet acknowledged.
    Domain->>Facade: publish_when_ready, or publish
    alt publish_when_ready: there is no room in the outbox
        Facade-->>Domain: waits, so that pressure slows the caller instead of failing it
    else publish: no room
        Facade-->>Domain: by its OfflineBehavior: Queue takes the buffer, or BufferFull when that is full,<br/>Fail is Busy, and Drop is counted
    end
    Facade->>Reader: PublishRequest(epoch, seq), numbered and kept until acknowledged

    Note over Pressure,Budget: Each second the PressureManager samples memory and sets the limit,<br/>from a floor of four frames up to a ceiling, and a limit below what is held evicts nothing.
    Pressure->>Budget: set_limit (or set_starved, under the floor)

    Note over Reader: A frame over 64 KiB is admitted before its body is read. A smaller one is read first,<br/>so acknowledgements are never stuck behind the frames they would make room for.
    alt it fits
        Reader->>Window: try_take, released when the topic accepts it
        Reader->>Budget: try_charge, refunded when its last holder drops it
        Reader->>Topic: on through the Listener and the session (broker/client_publish)
    else it does not fit
        Reader->>Reader: wait for admission, and tell the session ConnectionWaiting
        Note over Reader: A small publish waits while the reader reads on: an acknowledgement passes at once,<br/>and the first other frame is kept until it is admitted, then TCP pushes back.
        opt it fills the budget
            Budget-->>Pressure: exhausted: every reader waits, until what is held falls to three quarters
            Pressure->>Pressure: says so, with the five largest holders
        end
        alt room is made
            Reader->>Topic: admitted, and on it goes
        else the session judged it stalled and spared it (broker/slow_subscriber_stall)
            Reader-->>Facade: PublishResponse(Err, Unavailable, origin): refused for now, and it reads on
            Note over Reader: A large one's body is read past under one global slot, so the budget<br/>is passed by at most one frame while connections refuse.
            Facade->>Facade: kept in the outbox, a Deferral, one resend at a time,<br/>its wait doubling from 1 s to 30 s
            Facade->>Reader: PublishRequest, the same epoch and seq
        end
    end
    Topic-->>Facade: PublishRequestAck(id): it leaves the outbox and makes room, which wakes a waiting caller
